# The browsers the client bundle is compiled and prefixed for.
#
# This is Next's own default list, `MODERN_BROWSERSLIST_TARGET`, written down.
# Next looks for a browserslist config before falling back to that constant, so
# for the JavaScript this file says exactly what Next would have decided on its
# own. Writing it down is what makes it available to autoprefixer, which
# otherwise prefixes for browserslist's defaults, a different and older set
# than the one the JavaScript is built for.
#
# It cost more than that once. The app was compiled by Babel while it was on
# StyleX's Babel plugin, and `next/babel` leaves `@babel/preset-env` to ask
# browserslist. With no list to find, preset-env compiles for nothing in
# particular, which means ES5: 1.4MB of extra JavaScript across the bundle and a
# regenerator shim around every `await` in the app. The compiler is SWC again
# and that trap is gone, which leaves this file with one job rather than two.
#
# The cost of writing a default down is that it can drift from the default. If
# Next moves `MODERN_BROWSERSLIST_TARGET`, this pins the old one until somebody
# copies the new one across.
#
# It lives at the repo root rather than in `frontend/` because the file a build
# picks up depends on which directory it started in, and `pnpm --filter frontend
# build`, a Vercel build and an editor plugin do not agree on that. See
# `docs/stylex.md`.
chrome 64
edge 79
firefox 67
opera 51
safari 12
